            "children": [
              {
                "id": "nux4r85dosoyz6p",
                "text": "Foundational Concepts of Electrochemistry: Summary"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3cmhlp5sp0mq5yo",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Oxidation is the loss of electrons and/or an increase in oxidation number. Reduction is the gain of electrons and/or a decrease in oxidiation number. The species that is reduced is also called the oxidizing agent and the species that is oxidized is also called the reducing agent."
              }
            ],
            "id": "v6voewzqi5t3te1",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Oxidation-reduction reactions can be balanced using the half-reaction method. The redox reaction is split into oxidation and reduction half-reactions, which are then balanced separately. Each of the half-reactions must have the same number of each type of atom and the same total charge on each side of the equation. Charge is balanced in oxidation half-reactions by adding electrons as products; in reduction half-reactions, charge is balanced by adding electrons as reactants. The number of electrons gained by reduction must equal the number of electrons lost by oxidation when combining the two half-reactions to determine the overall balanced equation."
              }
            ],
            "id": "jwhkd889thwgkon",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Balancing oxidation-reduction reaction equations in aqueous solutions often requires adding oxygen and/or hydrogen. Acidic, aqueous solutions contain hydrogen ions and water, so H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O is added to balance oxygen and H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " is added to balance hydrogen. Basic solutions contain both hydroxide ions and water; they can most easily be balanced by first balancing the equation in acidic solution, and then adding hydroxide ions to both side of the equation in the quantities needed to convert all the OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "-"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions to H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O."
              }
            ],
            "id": "pc2fn90mtr4rj3w",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4ebflowue77bvqm",
                "text": "End of Lesson Self-Assessment"
              }
            ],
            "id": "kkuajwpgnajmm8n",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Before you take the Checkpoint, please evaluate your understanding of the learning objectives for the module "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Foundational Concepts of Electrochemistry"
              },
              {
                "text": " and use the My Response link below to ask any questions you might have about the material that you would like the instructor to address. Your questions can be for clarification of the material or further insight. Your self-evaluation and questions may also shape the discussions in class."
              }
            ],
            "id": "142h7nuj3odptvc",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Your responses will not be graded, but will be available for your instructor."
